e bit safer but that's a very  minor improvement for the bishop and it  would have been a lot better to instead  improve one of your other pieces for  example if you play the move d4 you're  giving the other bishop a whole new  diagonal to work on right he gains  access to five new squares which is a  lot better than just protecting one  piece that wasn't even under attack so  it's good to help your pieces realize  more of their full potential but it's  also good to spend about one move on  each piece and then move on to the next  piece because the amount you can improve  this bishop here which is like at 0%  versus this Bishop which is already at  like 75% power there's a lot more you  can improve this guy by a lot more than  you can improve this guy now three more  quick principles about the opening the  first one is that we like to control the  center of the board and we're going to  have a lesson on the center on a  different time on the YouTube channel  but the long and short of it is this the  center is the crossroads of the  chessboard right a lot of different  files ranks and diagonals shoot through  the center from the center your pieces  can move in any direction and go to any  sort of side of the board if you have a  knight here in the middle of the board  and the action is over here on the Kings  side then I can hop over there quickly  and join the fight if you have a fight  over here then I can quickly hop over to  that side and join the fight over there  so it's very flexible to have your  pieces in the center  number two is that you don't want to  make a lot of pawn moves in the opening  and if you think about it in terms of  improving your pieces how many squares  do your pawns control at the start of  the game exactly they control two  squares right and what is the maximum  number of squares upon can ever control  a pawn can only ever control two squares  right if we move our pawn to e4 well  it's nice that we put our pawn in the  center but he still is only controlling  two squares it's true that the quality  of the squares that he's controlling is  gotten better because now he's attacking  squares in the center and helping us  control an area of the board that we  want to control but still he was already  controlling two squares back at home  right so there's not as much to gain by  pushing your pawns out the main thing  you gain by pushing your pawns forward  is releasing your pieces so you only  need to make two pawn moves to get all  your pieces out you move your two Center  pawns like this for example and that  releases both of your bishops your  Knights can come out by themselves  because they jump the Queen doesn't have  to go out too far at the start of the  game and then the last principle of the  opening is that you want to get castled  and we say that the opening is over when  you've castled your King you've  developed all of your minor pieces your  bishops and your Knights and your rooks  are connected that means your books are  sort of attacking each other here and  looking at each other and this would be  a perfect opening for white developing  all of his pieces meanwhile black was I  don't know jumping as knight back and  forth in circles and not making any  progress this would be a perfect setup  for white in the opening now here is a  sample middle game position that I sort  of invented and it vaguely resembles or  right Lopes but the middle game is kind  of the largest and hardest to define of  the three parts of the game but in  general what you're trying to do in the  middle game is two things you're trying  to take pieces you're trying to win  material take more of their guys than  they can take of yours and number two is  sometimes you make checkmating attacks  against their king okay you can also try  to  saddle them with weaknesses but that's a  little bit of an advanced concept so to  aid you in winning material which is the  really the main thing that happens in  the middle game is that you trade pieces  and you try to get more there's all  sorts of things called tactics like  forks pins skewers discovered attacks  and those things are in our chests  tactics section on our chests comm  YouTube channel so you can look up those  things those are tools to help you in  material but basically in the middle  game you fight for a while and trade off  pieces and you reach the endgame when  you've traded enough pieces for your  King to be brave enough to come out from  his castle and fight so the endgame is  really different in that the rules of  some of your pieces changed drastically  and those two pieces are your pawns and  your king a big thing about the endgame  is that your King is now able to come  out into the game and join right the  king we never tried to activate him at  the start of the game because if you  activate your King by running into the  middle where he's strongest then you get  checkmated because there's too many  enemy pieces running around but in the  endgame when we've traded off pieces  with only a couple pieces left the King  comes out in fights  so we centralize our King and the other  thing we try to do is create and promote  passed pawns so a passed pawn is like  this pawn because no enemy pawn can halt  him in his advance to the other side of  the board when a pawn reaches the end of  the board it can be promoted and so that  is the goal of the endgame you activate  your King and all your pieces of course  and you try to promote your pawns and  this is a passed pawn for white because  only the enemy pieces but not pawns can  stop him and this would be a passed pawn  for black for the same reason so there  you have it in the opening you try to  bring out your pieces control the center  and castle your King once you've done  those things you enter the middle game  where you try to win material and make  favorable trades and you reach the  endgame when enough pieces have been  traded off for your king to be activate  and you 